## Lore

A Road Sentinel is a lone driver of the broken highways who has made himself
the protector of the people living along them: part vigilante, part
masterless knight on wheels. He owns little beyond his vehicle and his
weapons, and he spends his life moving from one fight to the next.

Sentinels hunt Roadgangers, bandits, slavers and monsters, and they are the
nearest thing to help that many Outback settlements ever see. Toward
farmers and travelers they are gentle and often selfless. Toward their
enemies they are pitiless: they attack on sight, shoot to kill, and strip
the wreck for fuel, ammunition and parts afterwards. They see the settlers
they defend as better people than themselves and never quite feel at home
among them.

Most have crossed the Outback end to end before they turn twenty-one, and
few live past thirty-five. They travel alone, in pairs, or for a while with
other adventurers.

## GM Notes

Not applied by the sheet: +1 melee attack/action when behind the wheel of a
vehicle; a successful roll with impact halves crash damage, and in an M.D.C.
smash-up the character takes S.D.C. and Hit Point damage rather than M.D.;
the three sharpshooting rules in the ability text.

Vehicle: a motorcycle and a car, or a pick-up truck or jeep-type vehicle,
plus $30,000 to spend on improving his own vehicles. Whatever is not spent
on them is lost, not kept as cash. The vehicle carries very basic tools; a
better tool set comes out of the vehicle budget.

Starting kit beyond what the sheet lists: one or two M.D. weapons and a
couple of S.D.C. weapons matching the character''s W.P.s, a camp stove, three
gasoline cans, a satchel, a box of matches and whatever else the G.M.
allows.

Money is 1D6x100 in cash plus 3D6x100 in saleable or tradeable items.
Australian dollars equal credits.

The book prints "(+20%)" on a line of its own under Outback Combat Driving
in the skill list. It may belong to the Motorcycle line above it, which is
printed "Pilot: Motorcycle or (+20%)"; a G.M. may allow a Hovercycle there.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'road-sentinel');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'road-sentinel';

